--TEST--
`Core\Zip::extract` judges the whole archive before it creates anything, so a hostile entry refuses with nothing on the disk and no destination — `rule:security/path-scope-canonicalise-then-prefix`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `notes/one.txt` beside `../../etc/passwd`: an archive that is harmless right
// up until somebody extracts it, which is where PHP's shape left the check.
bytes $traversing = Core\Encoding::fromBase64("");

// The refusal is the reader's, so it is the same sentence `entries` and `read`
// answer with — an extraction is not a second place where a name is judged.
try {
    Core\Zip::extract($traversing, "out");
    echo "extracted\n";
} catch (ParseError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// And it refused before it reached a door: the destination the call named was
// never created, so an archive that is refused leaves nothing at all behind —
// not even the empty directory it would have written into.
if (Core\IO::isDir("out")) {
    echo "out: created\n";
} else {
    echo "out: never created\n";
}

// The harmless entry of that archive is not extracted on its own either, for
// the reason `read` will not answer it: an archive carrying one traversing
// entry is not a well-meaning archive with a bad row in it.
if (Core\IO::exists("out/notes/one.txt")) {
    echo "out/notes/one.txt: written\n";
} else {
    echo "out/notes/one.txt: never written\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
Core\Zip refuses an entry when the archive is read: it traverses out of the archive with a `..` component: "../../etc/passwd" (rule:security/path-scope-canonicalise-then-prefix). The refusal is the archive's rather than the extraction's, so a program that walks the entries itself has nothing left it could have checked.
out: never created
out/notes/one.txt: never written
